{
  "id": "harf_i_tarif",
  "baslik": "The Definite Article: al-, as-, at-... Why Is It Pronounced Differently?",
  "seviye": "beginner",
  "sure": "10–15 minutes",
  "on_kosul": "Familiarity with the Arabic alphabet",
  "aciklama": "In Arabic, the definite article 'al-' changes before certain letters. In this lesson, you will learn why it changes and which letters cause it to change.",
  "bolumler": [
    {
      "id": "giris",
      "tip": "bilgi",
      "baslik": "What Is the Definite Article?",
      "icerik": "In Arabic, the prefix 'al-' (ال) makes a word definite, like 'the'. You hear it every day: ar-Rahman, as-Salam, al-Malik... But why does 'al-' sometimes become 'ar-' or 'as-'? The reason is simple.",
      "ornek": {
        "dogru": "Ar-Rahman ✓",
        "yanlis": "Al-Rahman ✗",
        "aciklama": "Because Ra is a sun letter, the 'l' sound changes to 'r'."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "kural",
      "tip": "kural",
      "baslik": "The Basic Rule: Where Does Your Tongue Touch?",
      "icerik": "The rule is practical: if your tongue touches the roof of your mouth or your upper gums when you pronounce the letter on its own, it is a SUN letter—the article is assimilated. If it does not, it is a MOON letter—the article stays as it is.",
      "hafiza_ipucu": "Shamsiyya means 'sun' (shams): the tongue moves forward, like the brightness of the sun. Qamariyya means 'moon' (qamar): the tongue stays back, like the quiet moon.",
      "gorsel_aciklama": "al + ش (Shin) → ash-shin | al + ب (Ba) → al-ba"
    },
