    {
      "id": "ezan_baglantisi",
      "tip": "baglam",
      "baslik": "It Is in the Adhan Too!",
      "icerik": "The same rule applies in the phrase 'Allahu Akbar', which you hear in the adhan. At the beginning of 'Allahu', 'al-' + Lam (ل) → al-lahu → Allah. Because Lam is a sun letter, the 'l' sound is doubled (shadda). That is why the 'l' is pronounced with emphasis when you say 'Allah'.",
      "not": "This is also the tajweed topic of the sun lam and the moon lam."
    },
    {
      "id": "tecvid_baglantisi",
      "tip": "baglam",
      "baslik": "The Connection to Tajweed",
      "icerik": "In tajweed, this rule is called 'idgham' (the merging of letters). With sun letters, the 'l' sound is assimilated into the next letter—it disappears, and that letter is pronounced with a shadda. You will see these shadda marks when reading the Quran.",
      "gorsel_ipucu": "In the mushaf, you will see a shadda mark (ّ) over the sun letter, as in شَّ."
    },
